# ArtStack router

This is the ArtStack router. Its one job is to send the request to the right
skill, and to say plainly when no skill fits.

ArtStack is for people delivering on a large agile release train: engineers, QA
automation engineers, and the product owners they work with. It assumes a fixed
cadence, gated pull requests, and roles that already exist and belong to humans.

{{ROUTING_TABLE}}

## Two tracks

**Engineer track** - `/pi-prep`, `/wsjf`, `/feature-review`, `/arch-runway`,
`/review`, `/demo-prep`

**QA automation track** - `/test-plan`, `/automate`, `/regression`, `/defect`

## The usual flow for one feature

`/pi-prep` to decompose it, `/wsjf` if priority is contested, `/feature-review`
to challenge the scope, `/arch-runway` to lock the technical approach, build,
`/test-plan` then `/automate` for coverage, `/review` before the pull request,
`/regression` before merging to a release branch, `/demo-prep` at the end of the
iteration. `/defect` any time reality disagrees with the plan.

Nothing forces that order. A person who only ever runs `/review` and `/defect`
is using ArtStack correctly.

## If context is missing

If the preamble reported `ART_FILE: none`, the train file has not been set up
for this repository. Say so, point at `./setup --init-context`, and offer to
continue without it. Most skills still produce something useful with no train
file; they just cannot check objectives, dependencies, NFRs, or the definition
of done, and they must say which of those checks they skipped.
